"text": "Now the hard part. You can't rehearse a jump like this in a real furnace. So Figure says it trained a new AI model in simulation. Here's how that kind of training generally works. A virtual robot tries the jump, and the attempt gets a score. Did it stay balanced? Push off cleanly? Land on target? The software nudges the controls toward whatever scored well, and it tries again, over and over, in many virtual copies at once. Virtual robots can fall on their faces all day, and nobody files an incident report. Figure says it used stunt performers' movements as a reference, a style guide for a good leap. The result is called a policy: a learned rule that turns what the robot senses into motor commands, many times a second. If a foot lands a little early, the sensors catch it, and the policy adjusts the knee and ankle before the robot tips. Not a wish, not a plan. More like a very well-rehearsed reflex.",
